1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,337
Most AI coding tools pull projects
into the agent.

2
00:00:03,337 --> 00:00:05,244
Patchwork reverses that.

3
00:00:05,244 --> 00:00:08,113
It is a browser canvas

4
00:00:08,113 --> 00:00:12,858
with no embedded chatbot, API key,
extension, or separate MCP server.

5
00:00:12,858 --> 00:00:15,070
Everything stays local-first.

6
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:19,199
Files and checkpoints
live in this browser.

7
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:23,545
A human edits code, watches the preview,
resets, and exports a ZIP.

8
00:00:23,545 --> 00:00:28,264
Inside ChatGPT, Codex discovers
ten WebMCP tools

9
00:00:28,264 --> 00:00:31,993
registered by the top-level page.

10
00:00:31,993 --> 00:00:35,741
It requests context, then reads
only the files it needs.

11
00:00:35,741 --> 00:00:39,531
The page returns paths, content, sizes,
and the observed revision.

12
00:00:39,531 --> 00:00:43,283
Now Codex updates three files
in one atomic write.

13
00:00:43,283 --> 00:00:47,329
Patchwork validates the full batch,
creates an automatic checkpoint,

14
00:00:47,329 --> 00:00:52,029
applies every change together,
and increments the revision once.

15
00:00:52,029 --> 00:00:54,029
The interface responds immediately.

16
00:00:54,029 --> 00:00:58,630
A WebMCP receipt names the operation,
the changed files are highlighted,

17
00:00:58,630 --> 00:01:00,831
and the preview reveals Roamly.

18
00:01:00,831 --> 00:01:04,612
Codex then inspects compiler
and runtime diagnostics.

19
00:01:04,612 --> 00:01:10,322
A checkpoint can restore the prior state,
and export remains under human control.

20
00:01:10,322 --> 00:01:14,433
This is why WebMCP matters:
instead of guessing at pixels,

21
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:19,672
the agent gets schemas, revisions,
diagnostics, and atomic operations.

22
00:01:19,672 --> 00:01:24,243
Patchwork is independent and is not
affiliated with or endorsed by OpenAI.
